    <text type="letter">
        <body>
            <div type="writingSession">
                <pb n="1"/>
                <opener>
                    <dateline><date when="1947-05-01">May 1, 1947</date></dateline><salute>Dear <rs
                            ref="#tillich_person_id__1928" type="person">Paul</rs>:</salute>
                </opener>
                <p>I suggest that you obtain from the Registrar&apos;s Office an exact statement
                    concerning the academic record that <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__4959"
                        type="person">Chares Ives</rs> made while he was in the Seminary, together
                    with the date when he received our B. D. degree.<note type="ea">Der Absatz ist
                        am linken Rand mit zwei dicken roten Linien markiert, die mit einem
                        handschriftlichen Vermerk <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1928" type="person"
                            >Tillichs</rs> am unteren Blattrand verbunden sind: <q>Would you be so good to do this
                            for me? <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1928" type="person"
                            >Tillich</rs></q>. Links neben diesem Vermerk, von fremder Hand:
                                <q><unclear>Bt</unclear> Thesis – <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1382"
                                type="person">Nestorius</rs> a-, B. D., <date when="1939-05-23"
                                    ><unclear>May</unclear> 23, 1939</date></q></note></p>
                <p><rs ref="#tillich_person_id__4959" type="person">Charles</rs> is a very
                    attractive and genial young man, who was <del rend="s">equally</del>
                    <add place="superlinear">eagerly</add> interested in philosophical and
                    theological problems during his days here. (He was a friend of <rs
                        ref="#tillich_person_id__4961" type="person">Steve Hartshorne</rs> and
                    belonged to the <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__4961" type="person">Hartshorne</rs>
                    cult – remember?)</p>
                <p>Unfortunately I do not know much at first hand about the work he has done in the
                    parish since his graduation from Union. I do know, however, that he got into
                    trouble in his first charge in <rs ref="#tillich_place_id__726" type="place"
                        >Connecticut</rs> because he combatted con<del rend="s">troversial</del><add
                        place="superlinear">ventional</add> ideas of immortality. I believe that it
                    stirred up such a controversy, that he finally had to resign. His attack on the
                    idea of immortality followed well-known Tillichian lines, but apparently with
                    youthful American indiscretion instead of with irenic, Tillichian charity.</p>
                <p>This is all I can tell you about the young man.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rend="hand"><rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1624" type="person"
                            >Dave</rs></hi><lb/><rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1624" type="person"
                            >DAVID E. ROBERTS</rs></signed>
                </closer>

            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
